<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Sardarabad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Sardarapat · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus-campaign">Caucasus campaign</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Sardarabad was fought from 21 to 29 May 1918 west of Erivan, when Armenian forces under generals Movses Silikyan and Daniel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bek">Bek</a>-Pirumyan stopped an Ottoman offensive into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Ararat</a> plain. Together with the battles of Bash Abaran and Karakilisa, it saved the Armenian heartland around Erivan, and the Armenian National Council declared independence on 28 May 1918. It is commemorated by a memorial complex at Sardarapat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sardarabad.jpg" alt="Battle of Sardarabad" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armenian_army_before_Sardarapat_battle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, newly forming Armenian detachments replaced Russian forces retreating from the Caucasian Front. The Ottoman government set out to regain the territories the Russian Army had occupied and to invade Eastern Armenia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>, intending to seize Russian Transcaucasia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil wells</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. The German government, its ally, objected and refused to help. The Ottoman Third Army attacked in February 1918, took the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> on 25 April and Alexandropol on 15 May, leaving only a small area unconquered, into which hundreds of thousands of Armenian refugees fled.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> the city council voted on 18 May to hand the city over without resistance. The Special Committee headed by Aram Manukian overturned the decision and had the defences fortified, with the help of as many as 1,500 residents. Catholicos <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> refused to leave Etchmiadzin and encouraged resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Ottoman 36th Division, with cavalry and 1,500 Kurdish fighters, numbered 7,500 to 10,000 men with 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>. The Armenian forces formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> detachment under Major-General Movses Silikyan; Colonel Daniel Bek-Pirumyan commanded the troops at Sardarabad itself.</p><p>On 21 May the Ottoman 108th Infantry Regiment attacked from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arak-iran">Araks</a> railway station. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> withdrew, and that evening a detachment under Zihni Bey took Sardarabad and advanced to within 20 km of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>. The Ottoman advance along the railway threatened to cut off the Armenian troops and as many as 100,000 Western Armenian refugees, but the artillery battery of Captain Khoren Igitkhanyan at Artashar pinned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in place. Silikyan and his chief of staff, Colonel Alexander Vekilyan, drew up a plan to drive the Ottomans out of Sardarabad by counter-attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Ottoman forces were routed and thrown back 50 to 65 km. Silikyan wished to press on to Alexandropol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, but was ordered to stop because of negotiations between the Ottoman leadership and the Armenian National Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>; ammunition was nearly exhausted and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had received reinforcements. Together with the battles of Bash Abaran and Karakilisa, the victory allowed the Armenian National Council to declare the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-batum">Treaty of Batum</a> of 4 June 1918 was harsh, but the republic held out until the Ottomans withdrew at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><p>The battles are known in Armenian historiography as the Heroic battles of May. Soviet historians gave Sardarabad little attention until after Stalin's death. A monument and park near the battlefield was completed in 1968.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Sardarabad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As the Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic broke up, the Armenian National Council declared independence on 28 May 1918, just as Armenian forces halted an Ottoman offensive at <b>Sardarabad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
